---
import HomePage from "./GatsbyHomeParity.astro"
import { getRootPageData } from "../lib/root-pages.mjs"

interface Props { pathname: string; locale: string; title: string; allRoutes: Array<any> }
const { pathname, locale, title, allRoutes } = Astro.props
const prefix = locale === "en" ? "" : `/${locale}`
const page = getRootPageData({ pathname, locale, title, routes: allRoutes })
const jsHello = "var name = \"Danger\"\nconsole.log(\"Hello, \" + name)"
const javaHello = "String name = \"Danger\";"
const tsHello = "var name: string = \"Danger\"\nconsole.log(\"Hello, \" + name)"
type LinkCard = [heading: string, body: string, href: string]
type DocsItem = { pathname?: string; title: string; items?: DocsItem[] }
type DocsGroup = { title: string; items: DocsItem[] }

const flattenDocsItems = (items: DocsItem[]): Array<{ pathname: string; title: string }> => items.flatMap(item => item.pathname ? [{ pathname: item.pathname, title: item.title }] : flattenDocsItems(item.items || []))
---
{page.kind === "home" ? <HomePage locale={locale} /> : <>
  {page.kind !== "empty" && page.kind !== "dt" && <section class="hero"><h1>{page.heading || page.title}</h1><p>{page.intro || page.subtitle}</p>{page.lead && <p>{page.lead}</p>}</section>}
  {page.kind === "empty" && <p>This page is intentionally left blank</p>}
  {page.kind === "community" && <section class="content-width"><div class="raised main-content-block"><h2>{page.headline}</h2><p>{page.sectionIntro}</p><div class="feature-grid">{page.cards.map(([heading, body, href]: LinkCard) => <article class="feature-card raised"><h2><a href={href}>{heading}</a></h2><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div></div></section>}
  {page.kind === "cheatsheets" && <section class="content-width"><div class="raised main-content-block"><div class="button-row"><a class="button primary" href="/assets/typescript-cheat-sheets.zip">{page.downloadLabel}</a></div><p>{page.intro}</p><p>{page.detail}</p><div class="feature-grid">{page.sheets.map((sheet: string) => <article class="feature-card raised"><h2>{sheet}</h2><p>{page.downloadSubtitle}</p></article>)}</div><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/assets/typescript-cheat-sheets.zip">{page.downloadTitle}</a></div></div></section>}
  {page.kind === "download" && <section class="content-width"><div class="raised main-content-block"><h2>TypeScript in Your Project</h2><p>Having TypeScript set up on a per-project basis lets you have many projects with many different versions of TypeScript, which keeps each project working consistently.</p><div class="feature-grid"><article class="feature-card raised"><h2>via npm</h2><p>TypeScript is available as a package on the npm registry as <code>typescript</code>.</p><pre><code>npm install typescript --save-dev</code></pre><pre><code>yarn add typescript --dev</code></pre><pre><code>pnpm add typescript -D</code></pre></article><article class="feature-card raised"><h2>Run the compiler</h2><p>All of these dependency managers support lockfiles, ensuring everyone on your team uses the same version of the language.</p><pre><code>npx tsc</code></pre><pre><code>yarn tsc</code></pre><pre><code>pnpm tsc</code></pre></article><article class="feature-card raised"><h2>with Visual Studio</h2><p>For most project types, you can get TypeScript as a package in NuGet for your MSBuild projects.</p><pre><code>Install-Package Microsoft.TypeScript.MSBuild</code></pre><p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/visualstudio/javascript/tutorial-aspnet-with-typescript">Install TypeScript through Visual Studio</a></p></article></div></div><div class="raised main-content-block"><h2>Globally Installing TypeScript</h2><div class="feature-grid"><article class="feature-card raised"><h2>via npm</h2><p>Use the <code>tsc</code> command anywhere in your terminal.</p><pre><code>npm install -g typescript</code></pre></article><article class="feature-card raised"><h2>via Visual Studio Marketplace</h2><p>Install TypeScript as a Visual Studio extension for MSBuild projects.</p><p><a href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=TypeScriptTeam.typescript-593">Visual Studio Marketplace</a></p></article><article class="feature-card raised"><h2>Compatible transpilers</h2><p>Babel, swc, and Sucrase can transpile TypeScript syntax while the TypeScript compiler continues to handle type checking.</p><p><a href={`${prefix}/tsconfig`}>Enable isolatedModules</a> when using external transpilers.</p></article></div></div></section>}
  {page.kind === "tools" && <section class="content-width"><div class="raised main-content-block"><div class="feature-grid">{page.cards.map(([heading, body, href]: LinkCard) => <article class="feature-card raised"><h2><a href={`${prefix}${href}`}>{heading}</a></h2><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div></div></section>}
  {page.kind === "why" && <article class="content-width raised main-content-block markdown"><p>{page.intro}</p><h2>What is JavaScript?</h2><p>Because TypeScript extends JavaScript, JavaScript is a good starting point. JavaScript is commonly used to create websites alongside HTML and CSS, and it now powers many services and applications outside the browser too.</p><p>JavaScript was originally designed to handle small interactions on websites. Today it powers front-end applications, build tools, servers, desktop software, and mobile apps.</p><h3>What do programming languages do?</h3><p>Programming languages are a way for humans and computers to communicate. Developers read code many more times than they write it, so language design is full of tradeoffs between brevity and explicitness.</p><pre><code>{jsHello}</code></pre><p>In JavaScript, the variable can later hold different kinds of values. In languages like Java, the declaration is more explicit about the kind of data it contains.</p><pre><code>{javaHello}</code></pre><h3>What is TypeScript?</h3><p>TypeScript contains all of JavaScript and then adds a little more. Existing JavaScript code is already valid TypeScript, and the extra syntax helps authors communicate what kinds of data move through the program.</p><pre><code>{tsHello}</code></pre><p>Those type annotations help humans and tooling understand the codebase. As projects grow, that extra explicitness reduces the cost of making changes safely.</p><h3>What problems can TypeScript solve?</h3><p>Without types, validating large JavaScript codebases requires more manual checking, more exhaustive tests, and more defensive review. TypeScript adds instant feedback while you work and makes wide-reaching refactors easier to trust.</p></article>}
  {page.kind === "dt" && <section class="content-width"><div class="raised main-content-block"><h1>{page.title}</h1><p><strong>{page.subtitle}</strong></p><p>{page.body}</p><p>You can read more on: <a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2020-12-16-npm-displays-packages-with-bundled-typescript-declarations">{page.linkLabel}</a>.</p></div></section>}
  {page.kind === "docs" && <section class="content-width"><div class="feature-grid">{page.groups.map((group: DocsGroup) => <article class="feature-card raised"><h2>{group.title}</h2><ul>{flattenDocsItems(group.items).slice(0, 10).map((item) => <li><a href={item.pathname}>{item.title}</a></li>)}</ul></article>)}</div></section>}
  {page.kind === "generic" && <section class="content-width"><div class="raised main-content-block"><p>{page.intro}</p></div></section>}
</>}